    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10040-026-03040-3",
    "abstract": "Abstract The ongoing degradation of Do\u00f1ana National Park (Spain), particularly its fragile sand-dune pond ecosystems, highlights the urgent need for effective and comprehensive conservation efforts. Santa Olalla Pond, once the only permanent pond in the park, has faced unprecedented drying events in recent years, reflecting the severe impacts of climate change and other hydrological challenges. This study addresses these challenges by developing a robust water level model for Santa Olalla Pond, using decadal hydrological data to analyze its behavior and predict its response to future climate change scenarios from 2030 to 2060. The results reveal a significant decline in the pond\u2019s hydroperiod due to reduced precipitation and rising temperatures, consistent with its transition into a temporary pond from 2022 onward. These findings underscore the ecosystem\u2019s increasing vulnerability and emphasize the critical importance of sustainable practices, improved water resource management, and proactive policies to preserve the ecological and cultural integrity of Do\u00f1ana National Park.",
    "field": "Hydrogeology",
    "problem": "This paper addresses the unprecedented drying of the once-permanent Santa Olalla Pond in Do\u00f1ana National Park by creating a robust hydrogeological model to predict its future response to climate change, a task complicated by hard-to-quantify groundwater-surface water interactions and changing pond bathymetry.",
    "method": "This study develops a robust water level model for Santa Olalla Pond using a calibrated water balance equation with decadal hydrological data to analyze its behavior and predict its hydroperiod response to future climate change scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a robust water level model for Santa Olalla Pond using decadal hydrological data to analyze its behavior and predict its response to future climate change scenarios from 2030 to 2060.",
